Requirements: 29   with obligations: 28   deliberately none: 1

[task-01] Obligation derivation reads one requirement at a time and produces obligations
    for it alone, so a requirement stated in two places yields two obligations that
    assert the same thing. On one observed task file, 34 requirements produced 71
    obligations. Every later stage runs once per obligation, so the duplication
    multiplies the work and the length of the report while adding nothing.
    -> dedupe-obligations-after-derivation  [functional/explicit]
       Add a post-derivation pass that recognizes when two obligations state the same
       requirement and links them instead of treating them as separate report content.
    -> link-surviving-obligation-to-all-stating-requirements  [functional/explicit]
       Ensure the surviving obligation is named by every requirement that stated it.

[task-02] The duplication is not a fault in the input. A mandate and its acceptance
    criteria naturally restate each other, and a requirement is often followed by a
    clause giving the reason for it. Both are ordinary ways to write a requirement, and
    both currently produce a second obligation.
    -> treat-restated-requirements-as-ordinary-input  [functional/explicit]
       Treat mandates and acceptance criteria that restate each other, and requirement
       clauses followed by their reasons, as ordinary requirement wording that can
       produce duplicate obligations.

[task-03] Add a pass that runs after derivation and recognises when two obligations
    state the same requirement. Its output is a link rather than a deletion: the
    surviving obligation is named by every requirement that stated it.
    -> link-duplicate-obligations-after-derivation  [functional/explicit]
       Run a post-derivation pass that recognizes duplicate obligations and emits a
       link-based result rather than deleting obligations.
    -> preserve-all-source-provenance-in-linked-obligation  [invariant/explicit]
       Carry forward the union of requirement links and source spans from everything
       merged into a linked obligation.

[constraint-01] The pass runs after obligation derivation, over the obligations
    derivation produced, rather than inside derivation.
    -> post-derivation-linking-pass  [functional/explicit]
       Run the duplicate-recognition pass after obligation derivation, operating on the
       produced obligations rather than inside derivation.

[constraint-02] Recognising a duplicate produces a link from a second requirement to an
    existing obligation. No obligation is deleted and no requirement text is discarded.
    -> duplicate-recognition-links-to-existing-obligation  [functional/explicit]
       Represent a recognized duplicate as a link from the later requirement to an
       existing obligation while preserving both the obligation and the requirement
       text.

[constraint-03] A linked obligation carries the union of the requirement links and the
    union of the source spans of everything merged into it, so it still traces to every
    piece of task text that produced it.
    -> linked-obligation-union-of-requirements-and-spans  [invariant/explicit]
       Carry forward the union of requirement links and the union of source spans for
       everything merged into a linked obligation.

[constraint-04] The links are typed fields in the schema-constrained response. A link
    stated in an obligation's description, rationale, or any other free-text field does
    not count as a link.
    -> typed-link-fields-only  [compatibility/explicit]
       Represent links as typed schema fields, so only typed fields count as links and
       free-text mentions do not.

[constraint-05] Two obligations about the same area of the change are not necessarily
    the same requirement. Where the judgement is uncertain, leave them separate.
    -> uncertain-duplicates-remain-separate  [human_review/explicit]
       Keep obligations separate when it is uncertain whether they express the same
       requirement.

[constraint-06] A requirement and a clause giving the reason for that requirement state
    one requirement, not two.
    -> reason-clause-counts-as-same-requirement  [functional/explicit]
       Treat a requirement and a clause giving its reason as one requirement for
       duplicate recognition.

[constraint-07] The obligations derivation produced, before any linking, are persisted
    in the review state. They are provenance rather than report content.
    -> persist-pre-link-derivation-in-review-state  [invariant/explicit]
       Persist the obligations derivation produced before linking in the review state as
       provenance rather than report content.

[constraint-08] Obligations derived for a requirement change only when that
    requirement's own relevant inputs change.
    -> derived-obligations-change-with-own-inputs  [invariant/explicit]
       Derived obligations change only when the requirement's own relevant inputs
       change.

[constraint-09] The links this pass produces are identical unless the derived
    obligations they were computed from change.
    -> links-stable-unless-derived-obligations-change  [invariant/explicit]
       The links produced by the pass remain identical unless the derived obligations
       they were computed from change.

[constraint-10] Two runs over byte-identical task text produce byte-identical review
    state, both for the derived obligations and for the linked result.
    -> byte-identical-task-text-yields-byte-identical-review-state  [regression/explicit]
       Two runs over byte-identical task text produce byte-identical review state for
       both the derived obligations and the linked result.

[constraint-11] Typed schemas are pydantic models, as the rest of the repository defines
    them.
    -> typed-schemas-are-pydantic-models  [compatibility/explicit]
       Typed schemas are implemented as pydantic models.

[constraint-12] Tests issue no live model calls.
    -> tests-avoid-live-model-calls  [error_handling/explicit]
       Tests issue no live model calls.

[exclusion-01] How finely a single requirement is split into obligations, which is #117.
    -> scope-split-granularity-handled-elsewhere  [human_review/explicit]
       How finely a single requirement is split into obligations is handled by another
       change and is not part of this task's delivered behavior.

[exclusion-02] Measuring how accurate the links are, which is #211.
    -> link-accuracy-measured-elsewhere  [human_review/explicit]
       Measuring how accurate the links are is handled by another change and is not part
       of this task's delivered behavior.

[exclusion-03] The wording of the decomposition prompt beyond what this pass requires,
    which is #205, #206 and #219.
    -> prompt-wording-beyond-this-pass-out-of-scope  [docs_config/explicit]
       The wording of the decomposition prompt beyond what this pass requires is out of
       scope for this task.

[exclusion-04] Whether an obligation needs test evidence at all, which is #148.
    -> preserve-test-evidence-scope  [invariant/explicit]
       Preserve the scope exclusion that whether an obligation needs test evidence at
       all is out of scope for this change.

[exclusion-05] Recovering requirements that an earlier run dropped.
    -> preserve-no-recovery-of-dropped-requirements  [invariant/explicit]
       Preserve the scope exclusion that recovering requirements dropped by an earlier
       run is out of scope for this change.

